Hon. members dispute this, but I may tell them that in our part of the [DOT]country and in nearly every small town where there 'are branches of the different banks all that business men are ever charged on any collection on drafts or notes is l of 1 per cent, and I think that any business
66}
man in this House will bear me out in this statement.

At this point may II be permitted to make a remark in regard to a question I put the other day to the Minister of Interior about the Shale Oil Company. I thought it was shell, but somebody said "Shale." I asked him if that company Jhad obtained a charter from the Government giving them the right to acquire oil-bearing lands of Western Canada. My hon. friend answered that a request had been made by that company and had been taken into consideration but had not been granted. I have not his exact words before me, but the answer was not very definite;
I do not know if they convey the impression that the company was barred from operation in Western Canada, or if for the time being the matter was not decided. I noticed in the press of Saturday or yesterday a despatch from Calgary in which it was stated that that company-called the Shale Company or the Royal Dutch Company-is actually operating in the West and boring it various places, and it is hinted that it is composed chiefly of gentlemen from Great Britain. I have heard of several 'lames in connection with the company, and particularly one piominent Canadian name, that of Sir Clifford Sifton. Does the hon. member know whether that company has

anything to do with the project which was discussed before the Imperial Conference? He will pardon me if I speak on this subject while he is giving such valuable information to the House, but I think he will remember that at the beginning of the war -he was not in the Government then-we passed legislation giving the Imperial Government a sort of prior right to all oilbearing lands. My hon. friend will, of course, understand the reason for this. The British navy depended considerably on a constant supply of oil during the war, and it was felt that it was necessary as much as possible to corral, so to speak, the oil-bearing lands in the various dominions. Now I would like to know if the company to which I have referred has anything to do with the project of which we have heard.
